# DESCRIPTION

`rio` (rio(1)) serves each of its windows' files in its tree, at `wsys/<id>/`, and the same files before `/dev` in the namespace of the window's programs.
They are Plan 9's rio's, less those of its draw device: there is no `mouse`, `cursor`, `window`, `winname` or `screen`.
A read that waits returns as soon as there is something; once the window has closed, reads return nothing.

: `cons`
  A read returns the next thing sent: a line, with its newline; what `^D` sent; or, in raw mode, a key's character.
  A read that asks for less gets it in parts.
  It waits until something is sent; `^D` on nothing typed makes a read return nothing, once.
  While `kbd` is open, reads wait.
  A write is shown at the output point, as text in UTF-8, a character split across writes shown when its rest comes.
: `consctl`
  A write of `holdon` or `holdoff` turns hold mode on or off, `rawon` or `rawoff` raw mode, each counted, as rio counts them; `rawon` ends hold mode, and sends what was typed.
  A write that begins with none of these fails as invalid.
  When the last channel open on it closes, both modes end.
  Reads return nothing.
: `kbd`
  While it is open, the window's keys come here and not to `cons`, as kbdfs(4)'s messages, each ending in a NUL: `k` and the keys held as a key goes down, `K` and those still held as one comes up, each as its rune without the modifiers, and `c` and the rune a key types, from the keymap's layer for Control or Shift if one is held.
  A read returns as many whole messages as fit, and waits if there are none.
: `label`
  The window's title, read and written as text.
: `text`
  The window's text, all of it.
  A write is shown as `cons`'s is.
: `wctl`
  A read returns the window's state: its content's left, top, right and bottom on the screen, `current` or `notcurrent`, and `visible` or `hidden`, each right aligned in 11 columns and followed by a space.
  The first read on a channel returns at once; each later one waits until the state has changed.
  A write is one of rio's verbs, each with parameters, below; one it can't take fails as invalid.
: `wdir`
  The window's directory: what the plumber is told a message came from.
  A write sets it, a path not starting with `/` taken from the directory there was, a newline at its end dropped, the name cleaned as cleanname's.
  `rc` writes it at a prompt after its directory changes.
: `winid`
  The window's id in `rio`, right aligned in 11 columns and followed by a space.

The top of `rio`'s tree has `wctl` too, which takes only `new` and reads the screen, as `0 0 width height`, then `nowindow` twice.

## wctl's verbs

A verb is followed by parameters, each a name and maybe a number; a number starting `+` or `-` is relative to the window's rectangle, and square brackets around numbers are allowed.

: `new` [`-dx` n] [`-dy` n] [`-minx` n] [`-miny` n] [`-r` x0 y0 x1 y1] [`-cd` dir] [`-hide`] [command ...]
  A new window, 600 by 400 or what the screen holds, each new one 16 further from the screen's top left than the one before, for ten; running `rc -c` with the command in `dir` (the writing window's directory, or `/`), or an interactive `rc` with none.
: `move` [`-minx` n] [`-miny` n]
  The window's top left; it keeps its size.
: `resize` [`-dx` n] [`-dy` n] [`-minx` n] [`-miny` n] [`-maxx` n] [`-maxy` n] [`-r` x0 y0 x1 y1]
  A new place and size.
: `top`, `current`
  Raised, and given the focus.
: `hide`, `unhide`
  Hidden, or shown again.
: `delete`
  The window closes, as its close box closes it.
: `scroll`, `noscroll`, `set`
  Taken, and nothing done.

Any verb takes `-id` n, which makes it about window n of `rio`'s.
A rectangle is kept on the screen, moved and then shrunk if it must be, and must be at least 100 wide and 24 high, and at most three screens.
These fail: a verb or parameter not known (`unrecognized wctl command`, `missing or bad wctl parameter`), text after the parameters of any verb but `new` (`extraneous text in wctl message`), a rectangle a window can't have (`bad rectangle in wctl request`), a verb other than `new` on `rio`'s own `wctl`, and `bottom`, since Radharc never lowers a window beneath another.

